          "final_text_tail": "py:41):\n- Added `__init__` initializing `self._alias_map` (an empty dict).\n- `add_alias(alias, target)` \u2014 registers/overwrites an alias mapping.\n- `remove_alias(alias)` \u2014 removes an alias; safe on nonexistent names.\n- `_resolve_alias(template)` \u2014 single-step resolution (no chaining): returns the target if registered, else the name unchanged.\n- Updated the default `get_source` to raise `TemplateNotFound` with the *resolved* (target) name.\n\n**All 7 subclasses** now call `super().__init__()` in `__init__` and resolve aliases at the start of their `get_source`/`load` methods (the actual entry points): `FileSystemLoader`, `PackageLoader`, `DictLoader`, `FunctionLoader`, `PrefixLoader`, `ChoiceLoader`, and `ModuleLoader` (which overrides `load` directly since it has no source access).\n\nKey behaviors verified:\n- Alias loading is identical to loading the target (same source, works through `Environment.get_template`).\n- Multiple aliases can point to one template.\n- Aliases do **not** chain (single-step resolution).\n- Removing an alias makes the name unavailable (raises `TemplateNotFound`).\n- A broken alias raises `TemplateNotFound` referencing the **target** name, not the alias.\n- Aliases work with empty strings and special characters.\n- Overwriting an alias re-points it.\n- `remove_alias` on a nonexistent alias is a no-op.\n\nNote: alias resolution happens at the loader level; the `Environment`'s template cache still applies as it does for any template name (orthogonal to this feature).",
